    <statement><p>Suppose <m>f \left( x, y \right) = 2 x^{2} + 2 xy + 2 y^{2}</m>, <m>P = \left( 2, 1
\right)</m>, and <m>\mathbf{u} = \left( \frac{3}{5},
\frac{4}{5} \right)</m>.</p>
<p>A. Compute the gradient of f.</p>
<p><m>\nabla f =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/> <m>\mathbf{i} +</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0002"/> <m>\mathbf{j}</m></p>
<p>Note: Your answers should be expressions of x and y; e.g. <lq/>3x - 4y<rq/></p>
<p>B. Evaluate the gradient at the point P.</p>
<p><m>\left( \nabla f \right) \left( 2, 1 \right) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0003"/> <m>\mathbf{i} +</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0004"/> <m>\mathbf{j}</m></p>
<p>Note: Your answers should be numbers</p>
<p>C. Compute the directional derivative of f at P in the direction <alert> u </alert>.</p>
<p><m>\left( D_{\mathbf{u}} f \right) \left( P \right) =</m> <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0005"/></p>
<p>Note: Your answer should be a number</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4x+2y</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>2x+4y</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>10</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
